WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.620
Why would a protein of the cell's spindle shrink the brain more than other organs?

2
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.360
Primary microcephaly is recessive.

3
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.420
The brain is small, and its layers are usually broadly in order.

4
00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:20.963
Most of its genes, such as ASPM, make proteins of the spindle pole.

5
00:00:22.017 --> 00:00:27.097
Radial glial progenitors are tall and thin, with a small patch facing the ventricle.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.840
In early divisions, the cleavage plane affects how that patch is shared.

7
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:35.493
Tilt the spindle, and one daughter can get it all.

8
00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:38.093
That can change what the daughters become.

9
00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:43.463
But a daughter without the patch can still divide, as a progenitor away from the

10
00:00:43.463 --> 00:00:44.463
ventricle.

11
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:50.493
In mouse embryos, knocking down Aspm tilted the division plane, and shifted

12
00:00:50.493 --> 00:00:52.433
divisions away from making progenitors.

13
00:00:53.517 --> 00:00:59.097
In mouse progenitors, delayed mitosis can also trigger a death that depends on p53,

14
00:00:59.757 --> 00:01:00.917
a sensor of cell stress.

15
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:04.987
And if a fault recurs round after round, it compounds.

16
00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:12.990
Predict. A child with no working ASPM has a markedly small brain, but mice lacking

17
00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.970
the gene have only a mild reduction.

18
00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:19.290
Ferrets have a folded cortex, rich in outer radial glia.

19
00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:23.530
Those are progenitors that have left the ventricle but keep dividing.

20
00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:25.990
Knock the gene out in a ferret.

21
00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:28.330
Less shrinkage, the same, or more?

22
00:01:34.683 --> 00:01:40.283
Much more. The authors traced it to radial glia leaving the ventricle too early.

23
00:01:41.143 --> 00:01:45.363
A second reading, still a hypothesis, is that more rounds mean more compounding.

24
00:01:46.417 --> 00:01:52.177
Now Zika. How do you show a virus causes a birth defect, without testing it in

25
00:01:52.177 --> 00:01:53.177
pregnant women?

26
00:01:53.817 --> 00:01:58.957
Epidemiology. Microcephaly rose after the virus, in time and place.

27
00:01:59.857 --> 00:02:02.597
That shows association, not cause or mechanism.

28
00:02:03.717 --> 00:02:08.077
Pathology. Virus was found inside a damaged fetal brain.

29
00:02:08.997 --> 00:02:11.577
On its own, that cannot show the virus did the damage.

30
00:02:12.617 --> 00:02:18.737
The laboratory. The virus infected human cortical progenitors most readily, killing

31
00:02:18.737 --> 00:02:20.277
some and slowing the rest.

32
00:02:21.157 --> 00:02:23.137
A dish cannot show the risk in a pregnancy.

33
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:30.130
Predict. The first study used an old Ugandan strain, kept for decades in the lab.

34
00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:31.970
What would a skeptic say?

35
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.820
That it might not act like the epidemic virus.

36
00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.760
Later work with epidemic strains agreed.

37
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.940
In 2016, a review against formal criteria concluded that Zika causes microcephaly.

38
00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:57.333
Two cautions. Most infections in pregnancy do not produce microcephaly.

39
00:02:58.173 --> 00:03:00.753
And the virus also damages tissue already built.

40
00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:08.947
So a spindle-pole gene, or a virus that finds the same cells, can leave too few

41
00:03:08.947 --> 00:03:09.327
neurons.
